 "name": "Heim Fox Mound",
 "place": "Middleton, Wisconsin",
 "region": "midwest",
 "blurb": "A fox or wolf effigy about 30 m long, built around 750–1000 CE, now under the trees of a Middleton neighbourhood near Lake Mendota.",
 "about": "Charles E. Brown, who spent his life recording Wisconsin's mounds, mapped this one in 1915. In 1937 the landowner, Ferdinand Heim, gave it to the Wisconsin Archeological Society, which still looks after it, and it was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 2004. The country around Madison's lakes once held well over a thousand mounds, and most were ploughed under or built over, so an animal still lying intact between the houses is a rare thing. It's one of the stops on Middleton's historic tour.",
